Luxstream bridge: clipboard and file transfer between the host and the Hyper-V VM.

Sunshine and Moonlight stream the *console* session.  Windows' own clipboard
redirection (RDP / Enhanced Session Mode) does not exist there, so there is
nothing in the streaming protocol that could carry a Ctrl+C from one machine to
the other.  This patch adds a small TCP service to the Moonlight client that
speaks a private framed protocol with luxstream-bridge.ps1 on the VM side.  The
service starts only when it finds luxstream-bridge.json in the user profile;
otherwise LuxBridge::start() returns nullptr and nothing else changes.

Four files change:
  app/app.pro                    backend/luxbridge.cpp/.h added to the build
  app/main.cpp                   LuxBridge::start() on the main thread
  app/backend/luxbridge.cpp      NEW - server, protocol, clipboard, send engine
  app/backend/luxbridge.h        NEW - Connection table and frame constants

The protocol (1 byte type, 4 byte little-endian length, payload):

  0x01 greeting            0x02 greeting reply ("OK" / "NO")
  0x10 clipboard text      0x11 clipboard image (PNG)
  0x12 file-group announce (JSON {count, ukupno})
  0x20 file header         (JSON {name, size, rel?})
  0x21 file chunk          (256 KB)
  0x22 file end
  0x30 PAUZA    - stop sending until the paste happens (JSON {name, size})
  0x31 NASTAVI  - the user pasted, send again               (empty)
  0x32 ODUSTANI - give the paused transfer up               (empty)

HOW A PASTE WORKS NOW (changed 01.11.2026)

The VM no longer puts a *path* on its clipboard.  It builds a clipboard file
group (FileGroupDescriptorW plus one FileContents stream per file) - the same
thing RDP and VMware do - so Explorer copies out of the stream we hand it and
never touches our file.  That removes two defects at once:

  * pasting onto another drive produced a 0-byte file, because Explorer's
    cross-volume "move" is copy-then-delete, and the copy ran while our file
    was still empty;
  * only one file per group could be pasted lazily, so a copied folder ended up
    stranded in Primljeno\.

A descriptor has to list the name and size of *every* file, so the receiver must
have all the group's headers before it can publish anything.  That is why this
patch sends the whole group's 0x20 headers up front, in one burst from
pollClipboard, before a single byte of content goes out.  pumpSend skips a
header that has already gone (TxItem::headerSent).  Without this the user could
only paste after every file but the last had been transferred - the entire
transfer, for a folder.

Drop files and single files are unaffected: their header is still sent by
pumpSend immediately before the first chunk.

0x30 / 0x31 (PAUZA / NASTAVI) belong to the VM's "hold the transfer until the
paste happens" design: the VM publishes the descriptor, stops sending, and waits
for its own FileContents stream to be read before pushing the bytes - the first
Read on that stream is the paste.  The host parses both frames and logs them,
but it never sends either: only the side that hands out the descriptor has a
reason to hold.  The host gives a paused transfer 15 minutes, the VM gives its
descriptor 10 (luxstream-bridge.ps1, -LazyCekajSec).

WHY 0x32 EXISTS (added 01.11.2026, same day)

0x32 (ODUSTANI - give up) closes the one hole that design left open, and it is
the reason plain text copy stopped working.

While a hold is up, BOTH sides stop polling their own clipboard: the VM sits in
its loop waiting for the paste, and the host returns early from pollClipboard
because txHeld is set.  For as long as the hold lasts, copying text does nothing
in either direction, and copying an image does nothing either.  And it does not
take a second mistake to keep it going: the host's m_LastHash is not carried
across connections, so when the hold expires, the link drops, and the host
reconnects, it announces the same file group again and the freeze starts over.
Seen in the field: two full ten-minute windows (14:56-15:06 and 15:12-15:22)
during which no transfer happened at all.

The clipboard can only hold one thing at a time, so the fix cannot be "never
interrupt a text copy".  It has to be: whatever the user copies next wins, and
the far side is told to let go.

  * VM side (luxstream-bridge.ps1): the outgoing clipboard poll is no longer
    skipped while a descriptor is up.  Text or an image there means the user
    moved on, so the group is abandoned and 0x32 goes out.  The same happens for
    content that arrives from the network while a descriptor is up: the
    descriptor is dropped and that content is applied at once, instead of being
    queued behind it.  Copied FILES are left alone - those mean the user is
    carrying on with the paused transfer, so the hold stays.
  * host side (this file): pollClipboard no longer returns early on txHeld.
    Text or an image on the clipboard cancels the queued job (cancelSend) and
    sends 0x32.  Copied files leave the hold untouched, as on the VM.

An older peer knows neither 0x32 nor the reason for it: it logs it as an unknown
frame type and ignores it, so the transfer is given up on one side only and the
other side degrades to the old waiting behaviour instead of breaking.

The two sides therefore upgrade independently in one direction only: a new VM
with an old host still works (headers arrive interleaved with data; the receiver
just builds its descriptor later, and the user pastes later), while a new host
with an old VM also works (the old VM ignores the extra up-front headers as
ordinary headers and keeps its per-file accounting).

Apply from the root of a moonlight-qt checkout:
  git apply moonlight-luxstream-bridge.patch
  # or: patch -p1 < moonlight-luxstream-bridge.patch

The patch is written against LF sources.  On a Windows box with
core.autocrlf=true the two NEW files are added with CRLF line endings, which is
harmless - but if that matters, disable the conversion:
  git -c core.autocrlf=false apply moonlight-luxstream-bridge.patch

This is not a cosmetic change: the client has to be recompiled for the bridge to
exist at all.  Luxstream-Moonlight-Host.exe ships the resulting Moonlight.exe
prebuilt, so an end user never applies this patch - it is here so that the
binary can be rebuilt from source.
